      "content": "I'll read the original test file and the CUT source to understand what needs to be improved.Now I'll create the improved test. The original file has many unused data provider methods and imports that clutter the single focused test. I'll strip everything down to just what's needed for `test_badLeapDayDates`.Now I'll compile-check the file.The script expects the file in the current working directory. I need to write it there directly.The improved test compiles successfully. Here's a summary of what was changed to improve understandability:\n\n1. **Removed ~130 lines of irrelevant data providers** (`data_samples`, `data_badDates`, `data_lengthOfMonth`, `data_prolepticYear_badEra`, `data_ranges`, `data_getLong`, `data_with`, `data_with_bad`, `data_temporalAdjusters_lastDayOfMonth`, `data_plus`, `data_plus_leapWeek`, `data_until`, `data_until_period`, `data_toString`) — none of them were used by the single test method.\n\n2. **Removed ~50 unused imports** — the original pulled in a wide range of temporal fields, units, and chronology types that had nothing to do with this test.\n\n3. **Renamed `data_badLeapDates` → `data_nonLeapYears`** — the new name directly states what the values represent (years that are not leap years), making the `@MethodSource` link self-documenting.\n\n4. **Added a class-level Javadoc** explaining *why* day 29 of December is invalid in these years — the calendar rule about the leap week extending December from 28 to 35 days.\n\n5. **Added a Javadoc on the data method** with the actual leap formula so a reader can verify the four years without consulting external sources.\n\n6. **Added an inline comment** in the test body explaining the semantic meaning of day 29 in December."
